  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — unchanged. They share nothing with [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  [1m4. Error handling and the fallback[0m

  Unknown hostnames resolve to [38;5;153mproduction[39m. That means opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight off disk ([38;5;153mfile://[39m, where [38;5;153mhostname[39m is
  [38;5;153m""[39m) yields [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m — byte-identical to today's behavior, which is the property I most want to
  preserve.

  The defensible opposite is failing loudly on an unrecognized host, so a misconfigured deploy can't silently talk to
  production. I'm recommending the quiet fallback because this is a login form whose [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is still a stub, and a hard
  failure would break the page for any host you haven't enumerated. Flag it if you'd rather it throw.

  [1m5. Testing and tooling[0m

  This repo has [1mno test runner, no linter, and no dependencies[0m — so there's no established pattern to follow, and I
  won't invent one silently. [38;5;153mdetectEnvironment[39m is the only real logic here and it is genuinely worth a test: it has four
  branches and it's the thing that decides which backend your users hit.

  Node ships [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, so a runner costs zero dependencies — but [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mwindow[39m/[38;5;153mlocation[39m, so testing it means
  having the module take an injectable global (a small seam) rather than reading [38;5;153mwindow[39m directly.
